1
00:00:00,000 --> 00:00:07,790
It's only relatively recently that designers have become aware of the need to be inclusive when designing products.

2
00:00:07,790 --> 00:00:10,650
But what does that mean exactly?

3
00:00:10,650 --> 00:00:17,840
Well, it simply means designing products that span economic, social and cultural barriers.

4
00:00:17,840 --> 00:00:20,731
It means making sure products are accessible -

5
00:00:20,731 --> 00:00:31,470
so that as many different types of people as possible can use them without any type of adaptation having to be made to the original design.

6
00:00:31,470 --> 00:00:35,500
Inclusive design is often linked with universal design

7
00:00:35,820 --> 00:00:38,610
although they are not quite the same thing.

8
00:00:38,610 --> 00:00:42,580
Universal design aims to make products that work for everyone

9
00:00:42,580 --> 00:00:46,594
and that includes considering the needs of people

10
00:00:46,594 --> 00:00:51,030
who have cognitive difficulties, which can present quite a challenge.

11
00:00:51,030 --> 00:00:57,450
Today, examples of successful inclusive design can be seen all around us.

12
00:00:57,450 --> 00:01:00,930
In workplaces it is common to see desks

13
00:01:00,930 --> 00:01:07,020
which can be adjusted to suit people of different heights or for wheelchair users.

14
00:01:07,020 --> 00:01:09,791
This still isn't always the case,

15
00:01:09,791 --> 00:01:15,730
however, and is one reason why office workers often suffer from back or neck problems.

16
00:01:15,730 --> 00:01:23,830
You'll find another example in the public toilets of countless hotels, airports and offices.

17
00:01:23,830 --> 00:01:30,310
Taps that you activate by sensor require no pressing or twisting movements.

18
00:01:30,310 --> 00:01:32,560
These are not only more hygienic

19
00:01:32,840 --> 00:01:38,110
they're also easier for people with dexterity or mobility issues.

20
00:01:38,110 --> 00:01:45,609
The tech industry has been criticised in the past for focusing too much on young consumers

21
00:01:45,609 --> 00:01:52,170
but this is changing. Many products are now designed with the elderly in mind.

22
00:01:52,170 --> 00:01:57,606
For example, it's well known that vision declines with age,

23
00:01:57,606 --> 00:02:03,043
and that we also become worse at distinguishing between similar colours;

24
00:02:03,043 --> 00:02:04,740
in particular, shades of blue

25
00:02:05,380 --> 00:02:10,950
which is why software designers rarely create interfaces with this colour.

26
00:02:10,950 --> 00:02:13,595
Motor skills also decline with age

27
00:02:13,595 --> 00:02:21,090
and some people have difficulty doing everyday things like picking up a cup or opening a door.

28
00:02:21,090 --> 00:02:26,631
This can also affect their ability to use a mouse or keyboard.

29
00:02:26,631 --> 00:02:31,710
So voice access is now a routine way of making commands.

30
00:02:31,710 --> 00:02:35,940
It's worth looking at the problems non-inclusive designs cause

31
00:02:35,940 --> 00:02:40,359
when not enough consideration is given to a range of users,

32
00:02:40,359 --> 00:02:44,890
as it can have a serious impact on people's lives.

33
00:02:44,890 --> 00:02:47,920
Access is one obvious example

34
00:02:47,920 --> 00:02:53,710
because it has such a huge impact on disabled people's independence.

35
00:02:53,710 --> 00:02:57,088
Not being able to access public transport

36
00:02:57,088 --> 00:03:06,740
because buses or trains are not wheelchair-friendly means many disabled people can't go out unless someone goes with them.

37
00:03:06,740 --> 00:03:09,643
Safety is another issue. Inexplicably,

38
00:03:09,643 --> 00:03:17,190
most cars are still crash-tested using a dummy based on an average-sized male.

39
00:03:17,190 --> 00:03:20,514
This has safety implications for all women,

40
00:03:20,514 --> 00:03:23,020
particularly those who are pregnant

41
00:03:23,360 --> 00:03:29,060
as the seatbelts worn by the dummy are not adapted to accommodate them.

42
00:03:29,060 --> 00:03:33,660
Over the past 100 years, workplaces in the UK have

43
00:03:33,760 --> 00:03:37,550
on the whole, become considerably safer.

44
00:03:37,550 --> 00:03:43,541
Employers are legally required to provide well-maintained personal protective equipment or PPE -

45
00:03:43,541 --> 00:03:51,030
anything from goggles to full body suits - to workers who need it, free of charge.

46
00:03:51,030 --> 00:03:54,266
But most PPE is designed to fit men.

47
00:03:54,266 --> 00:03:58,860
A recent report found that employers often think that

48
00:03:58,860 --> 00:04:00,332
when it comes to female workers,

49
00:04:00,332 --> 00:04:06,399
all they need to do to comply with this legal requirement is to buy jackets,

50
00:04:06,399 --> 00:04:09,230
for example, designed for a small man.

51
00:04:09,230 --> 00:04:12,764
The problem with this is that women can be tall

52
00:04:12,804 --> 00:04:18,887
and still have much smaller shoulders than the average man

53
00:04:18,887 --> 00:04:26,130
Ill-fitting PPE such as hi-vis jackets, vests and body armour can put women at risk.

54
00:04:26,130 --> 00:04:33,544
The report found that 95% of women said that their PPE often hampered their work

55
00:04:33,544 --> 00:04:39,970
and that this problem was worst in the emergency services, particularly the police.

56
00:04:39,970 --> 00:04:43,930
Another problem is related to comfort at work.

57
00:04:43,930 --> 00:04:51,850
A very common scenario in offices in summertime is to see women wrapped in blankets or wearing sweaters

58
00:04:51,850 --> 00:04:54,930
while the air conditioning is on high.

59
00:04:54,930 --> 00:04:57,120
Meanwhile the men are in shorts

60
00:04:57,120 --> 00:05:03,670
and T-shirts. This is due to differences in metabolic rates for men and women.

61
00:05:03,670 --> 00:05:09,832
There is a standard setting for air conditioning to be at a temperature of 21 degrees,

62
00:05:09,832 --> 00:05:17,920
designed to suit men, and in most modern offices it's not possible to turn the air conditioning up or down.

63
00:05:17,920 --> 00:05:20,370
This means that many offices

64
00:05:20,370 --> 00:05:27,720
which mainly employ women are wasting energy by having the air conditioning set too high.

65
00:05:27,720 --> 00:05:31,535
As you can see from the examples I've just mentioned,

66
00:05:31,535 --> 00:05:33,360
there are serious consequences for designs

67
00:05:33,360 --> 00:05:37,430
which don't consider the needs of all users. This is...
